<header><p class="st-kicker">kingdom · China, Tibet and Mongolia</p><h1 class="st-title">Kingdom of Dai (206 BC)</h1><p class="st-meta">206 BCE – 204 BCE · capital Dai</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">A northern kingdom of the Chu-Han war, first the place of exile for the Zhao king Zhao Xie and then the fief of Chen Yu, overrun by Han Xin.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p>In 206 BCE <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/xiang-yu">Xiang Yu</a> moved <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/zhao-xie">Zhao Xie</a>, the restored king of Zhao, north to the frontier region of Dai, with its capital at Dai near modern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yu-county-hebei">Yuxian</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hebei">Hebei</a>, so that <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/zhang-er">Zhang Er</a> could rule the Zhao heartland as the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/kingdom-of-changshan">Kingdom of Changshan</a>.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>In 205 BCE <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chen-yu">Chen Yu</a> drove out <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/zhang-er">Zhang Er</a> and brought <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/zhao-xie">Zhao Xie</a> back to Zhao; in thanks, Zhao Xie made Chen Yu King of Dai. Chen Yu stayed in Zhao as chief minister and governed Dai through his deputy <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/xia-yue">Xia Yue</a>. The kingdom was a frontier zone of herding and farming facing the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/xiongnu-empire">Xiongnu</a>.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p>In 205 BCE <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/han-xin">Han Xin</a> defeated and captured <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/xia-yue">Xia Yue</a>, and in 204 BCE he destroyed the Zhao army at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-jingxing">Jingxing</a>, where <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chen-yu">Chen Yu</a> was killed. Dai passed to the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/han-dynasty">Han Dynasty</a> and later became a Han kingdom held by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/liu-bang">Liu Bang's</a> son, the future <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/emperor-wen-of-han">Emperor Wen</a>.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><div class="st-emblem" role="img" aria-label="kingdom, 206 BCE – 204 BCE"><svg viewBox="0 0 100 100" aria-hidden="true"><path d="M22 72 V40 L36 54 L50 28 L64 54 L78 40 V72 Z"/><path d="M22 80 H78"/><circle cx="50" cy="24" r="3"/><circle cx="22" cy="36" r="2.5"/><circle cx="78" cy="36" r="2.5"/></svg><p class="st-emblem-type">kingdom</p><p class="st-emblem-region">China, Tibet and Mongolia</p><div class="st-span" aria-hidden="true"><span class="st-span-bar" style="left:55.60%;width:1.20%"></span></div><div class="st-span-axis" aria-hidden="true"><span>3000 BCE</span><span>1 CE</span><span>2000</span></div></div><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>206 BCE – 204 BCE</dd><dt>Century</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/3rd-century-bce">3rd century BCE</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>kingdom</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>feudal</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Dai</dd><dt>Rulers</dt><dd>Zhao Xie, Chen Yu</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Chinese ancestral religion</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Old Chinese</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd><a href="/countries/china">China</a></dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Dai (Zhao Xie), Dai (Chen Yu)</dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=-205&amp;focus=kingdom-of-dai-206-bc" aria-label="Kingdom of Dai (206 BC) on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="7394 987 640 512" role="img" aria-label="Map: where Kingdom of Dai (206 BC) lay"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-n" d="M7739 1235h0"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M7714 1243h0"/><path class="map-dot-ap" d="M7714 1243h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>Location approximate: shown in present-day China. <a href="/atlas?year=-205&amp;focus=kingdom-of-dai-206-bc">On the atlas &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></aside></div>

